+Spec: docs/superpowers/runs/2026-11-26-resurrect-seat-frictionless-restart/2026-11-26-resurrect-seat-frictionless-restart-design.md at head, including its `## Requirement baseline` (B1 to B4; no human scope changes).
+
+## Requirement baseline trace
+
+- B1 (undo of a closed tab brings back the original threads seat through ResurrectSeat). Production path: `ThreadsSource` diff calls `clone_wants`, which calls `resurrectable`. It emits `threads.resurrect_seat` for a pending Restore whose fact reads `Retired{reversible: true, keyed: false}` on a verified pane. `ThreadsExecutor::resurrect_seat` reads `replace` from the seat map at execution time, then calls `ThreadsPort::resurrect_seat`. The compose `lazy` port routes the call to `ServiceThreads::restore`, which sends `ServiceOperation::ResurrectSeat` with the host locator and `replace`. On Done or AlreadyResolved, `AcknowledgeResurrected` sets `threads_done` and resets the Ordinary Pending invitation rows in the same write. The existing restore launch then runs under its unchanged `threads_done` gate. Keyed reversible facts keep `UnretireSeat`, because `unretirable` now requires `keyed: true`, and legacy facts deserialize as `keyed: true`. Final facts keep succession. The NotFound, transient and definitive outcomes, the refusal meta, re-planning when replace or pane changes (via an op key carrying the count), the `resurrect_held` row and doctor finding, the undo preview window note and the windowed adopt warning all match spec §3. Delivered within the cluster.
+- B2 (frictionless Herdr restart). Production path: `rebind_wants` is added to `ThreadsSource::diff`. It emits once per seated active clone whose fact reads `Unresolved` at or after `incarnation:changed_at`, while the bound pane is live and no `rebind_refused` is recorded for the incarnation. `rebind_held_seat` goes through the compose port to the real adapter (`ResurrectSeat` with `replace: None`). `plan_resume` records `seat_unresolved:<clone>`. `keyless_start_gate` in `HerdrExecutor::start` defers keyless fresh starts inside the grace until the seat is placed in this incarnation. `RelaunchOccupant` keeps its 90 s grace. `restart_covered` counts a `launched:` meta for non-shell harnesses (§4.3). The `restart_held` status row and doctor check name the remedy in threads' `human` namespace. Every row of the spec §4 table is covered by the named reconcile tests (restart_keyed_*, restart_keyless_*, restart_shell_seat_*, restart_never_observed_*, restart_missing_pane_*). The end-to-end private-Herdr proof sits in tests/ and belongs to the seam and tests reviewers. Delivered within the cluster.
+- B3 (graph registers v4 through the upstream level option). `ServiceThreads::new` calls `PersistentServiceClient::new(...).with_level(GRAPH_SERVICE_LEVEL)` with V4. A refused registration maps to the named `level_refused` error, with no v3 or Notify fallback; `delivery_capability` no longer downgrades on `Unsupported`. The doctor `threads` check appends an upgrade step. Delivered. The pin itself (Cargo.lock, third_party) is outside this cluster.
+- B4 (the U2 rebind of a restart-held seat before relaunch; resume whenever a session exists). `rebind_held_seat` is `ResurrectSeat` with no replace, and `SeatAlreadyResolved` maps to `AlreadyResolved` (done). The resume argv and the resume path are unchanged. Delivered.
+
+## Correctness notes
+
+- `AcknowledgeResurrected` is chosen on `clone.keyless` in `ack_restore`, but on the fact in `resurrect_seat`. A graph-keyed clone whose seat threads holds keyless (its key reservation failed) therefore gets the invitation reset only when the resurrect call itself succeeds, not on the NotFound-then-Resolved acknowledgement path. The task 6 report already names this. Its effect is limited to re-issuing ordinary invitations in the 24 h to 7 d window for that rare clone.
+- The adapter forgets a definitive refusal from the client intent journal, so a retry under the same key is a fresh decision. Pending or busy calls stay journaled for replay. This matches the threads contract (a refusal rolls back and stores nothing) and is covered by `restore_retry_under_the_same_key_is_a_fresh_decision`.
+- `rebindable` and `restart_held` key only on an `Unresolved` fact read after `incarnation:changed_at`, with no other restart condition. For the whole incarnation, a seat that turns Unresolved for some other reason triggers one rebind. Threads answers NotFound, which is recorded as `rebind_refused`. After the grace the seat also shows a `restart_held` row whose remedy is an operator rebind, even though the cause was not a restart. One example is a keyed seat after `UnretireSeat`, until its key checks in. The task 13 report named it. The spec's emission rule reads this way literally, so this is status noise, not a spec miss.
+- `compose::lazy` now builds `ServiceThreads` only after `endpoint.host()` succeeds. A host-claim failure there is `ThreadsError::Rejected`, and `port_outcome` maps that to `Failed`, so every threads mutation now fails for good while the host claim is failing. Before, only the seat map had that dependency. In practice the claim fails only when the Herdr socket's parent directory does not exist.
+- The `restart_covered` extension (a `launched:` meta counts as had-agent) also applies outside a restart window. A graph-launched keyed agent with no captured session, whose seat reads Unresolved at any point after the last incarnation change, gets one `RelaunchOccupant` per incarnation. Clones with a captured session already behaved this way, so the extension follows the spec and introduces no new class of behaviour.
+- The lingering restore intent (task 2 concern): if an undo-restored keyless agent exits before it checks in, `ClearOnReattach` never lands and `start_needed` stays shut out. This is pinned as current behaviour by a test, and the window is narrow.
